# Chapter 1, part 1: the elements of a document, the two kinds of document,
# guidelines for large documents, and global and local formats.
.h2 The elements of a document [view-elements]
.ix Elements of a document
.ix Document, elements of
.ix Text, block, column, section
Every Strata document is built from four elements: text, blocks, columns and
sections.  A letter has one block with one column of text; a manual has
hundreds of sections.  The commands work on these elements.  Copy, Cut, Move
and Delete ask for the element in a pop-up that lists Text, Column and Block,
and Section in a structured document.

.table 10 50
Element | What it is
Text | Characters, words and lines inside a column.  The smallest unit.
Column | A vertical part of a block.  Every block has at least one.
Block | A unit of related text: a paragraph, a list item, a headline, a
 | table row.  The unit of most commands.
Section | A headline block followed by text blocks and, below them, the
 | subsections.  Only in a structured document.
.end

The elements nest: a section holds blocks, a block holds columns, a column
holds text.  A subsection is a section inside another section.  The Editor
draws the borders of the elements on the screen ({n:view-screen-text}).

.h3 What text is [view-text]
.ix Text (element)
.ix Character
.ix Word
.ix Attribute, text
.ix Highlight, text
Text is the characters of a document: letters, digits, punctuation, spaces,
tabs and the ends of lines.  It is the smallest element.  A character, a
word, a line or several paragraphs are text when they lie inside one column.
A marker (a date, a page number, a footnote, an equation, a picture) is part
of the text: it takes the place of one or more characters and moves with them
when the text is moved.  Markers are described in {x:view-markers} and, in
full, in {x:markers}.

Text has attributes.  Strata has four: normal, bold, italic and underscore.
They are set with {k:Ctrl-B}, {k:Alt-I}, {k:Ctrl-U} and
{k:Ctrl-N}, or with {k:Alt-F6} (the Set Attributes menu), and they can be
mixed (bold italic).  An attribute set for a stretch of text is the most
local format there is: no other setting overrides it.  The attribute under
the cursor is shown on the status line after the word {c:Attr:}.

A command that works on text prompts for a highlight: move the cursor over the
characters, then press {k:Enter} to accept or {k:Esc} to cancel.  The Editor
draws the highlighted characters in reverse video ({n:view-highlight}).

.h3 What a block is [view-block]
.ix Block (element)
.ix Block, separator line
.ix Paragraph
.ix Headline block
.ix Text block
A block is a unit of related text.  The usual block is a paragraph; a
headline, a row of a table, an item of a list, a line of an address and a
quotation are blocks as well.  Every document has at least one block.  A new
unstructured document is one empty block.

Strata keeps most settings per block: each block has its own justification,
indent, tabs, attribute and named format.  A document is reorganized by
cutting, copying and moving whole blocks.  {k:Ctrl-Home} and {k:Ctrl-End}
move the cursor to the start and to the end of the block (more exactly, of
the column of the block that holds the cursor).

{k:Enter} ends a line inside a block.  It does not end the block: the lines
stay in the same block and the cursor moves to the next line.  A new block
begins with {k:Ctrl-A} (the new block has the local format of the current
one), with {k:Ctrl-O} (the new block has the global format) or with the
Append command ({k:Alt-F2}).  {k:Ctrl-V} or {k:F2} divides the block in two at
the cursor.  Type a paragraph as one block and press {k:Enter} only where a
line must end (an address, a line of verse); the Editor wraps the text of a
block to the width of the screen.

The Editor marks the border between two blocks with a single horizontal line.
In a structured document the first block of a section is its headline block
and the others are text blocks.  A headline block can have a format of its
own, which depends on the level of the section ({n:view-levels}).

.h3 What a column is [view-column]
.ix Column (element)
.ix Table
.ix Column separator
.ix Multicolumn block
A column is a vertical part of a block.  Every block has at least one
column, and most blocks have exactly one: the whole block is that column.  A
block with several columns builds a table or a schedule: each row is a block
and each cell is a column.  The columns of a block lie side by side; each has
its own text, justification and tabs.

The Editor marks the border between two columns with a vertical line of dots
in the dim color of the borders (a colon in the terminal version).  To add a
column to the right of the current one, press {k:F8} and choose Add column, or
choose Column in the Append command.  To cut the current column in two at the
cursor, press {k:F8} and choose Split column here, or choose Column in the
Split command.  {k:Shift-Left} and {k:Shift-Right} move from one column to the
next.

.note The widths of the columns of a table come from the settings that the
  document holds.  A table keeps its columns and borders when the document
  is saved and retrieved.

.h3 What a section is [view-section]
.ix Section (element)
.ix Subsection
.ix Headline
.ix Section number
.ix Section tag
.ix Level of a section
.ix Level
.ix Peer section
A section is a group of related blocks: one headline block, which holds the
title of the section, and any number of text blocks after it.  A section can
have subsections, which are sections of the next lower level, each with a
headline and text blocks.  The section is the unit of reorganization: moving
a section moves its headline, its text and all its subsections.

The Editor marks a section with a double horizontal line above the headline
and draws the headline with its number in front of it ({c:2.1 Sales}).  The
Editor makes the numbers from the place of the section in the outline; they
are not typed and are not part of the headline text.  A section that moves
gets a new number at once.  The status line shows the number of the section
that holds the cursor after {c:Sect:}.

.h4 Levels [view-levels]
.ix Level, of a section
.ix Level, format
Every section has a level.  A section of level 1 is a main topic; a
subsection of a section of level 1 has level 2, and so on.  The level is the
count of the numbers in the tag of the section: the sections numbered 1, 2
and 3 have level 1, and the sections numbered 1.1, 2.3 and 3.1 have level 2.
Promote and Demote ({k:Alt-[} and {k:Alt-]}) change the level of a section and
of all its subsections.

The level selects the format.  The global settings give the headlines of each
level a format of their own (for example bold for level 1 and italic for
level 3), and the text blocks that follow a headline can have the settings of
its level.  See {x:view-formatting}.  {k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9} show the headlines
down to that level ({n:view-outline-keys}).

.h4 The shape of a section on the screen [view-section-shape]
The picture shows a short report in the X11 version.  The first block of the
document is the headline of section 1, with the double rule drawn between
sections.  Section 2 has the subsection 2.1, which has the subsections 2.1.1
and 2.1.2; each lower level is shifted to the right.

.pic pictures/view-structured.png 5.4 A structured document with its rules.

.table 17 43
What you see | What it is
Single line | The border between two blocks.
Double line | The border above the headline of a section.
{c:*} or {c:+} in the double line | The section is collapsed
 | ({n:view-outline-keys}).
Shift of 3 characters per level | The depth of the section in the outline.
Number before the headline | The tag of the section.
Dotted vertical line | The border between two columns of a block.
.end

.h2 Documents with sections and documents without [view-modes]
.ix Structured document
.ix Unstructured document
.ix STRUCTURED EDIT
.ix UNSTRUCTURED EDIT
.ix SECTION EDIT
.ix Mode of a document
.ix Structured edit
.ix Unstructured edit
A document is unstructured or structured.  The right end of the status line
shows which: {c:UNSTRUCTURED EDIT} or {c:STRUCTURED EDIT}.

.table 13 47
Kind | What it is
Unstructured | A run of one or more blocks, with no sections and no
 | headlines.  A memo, a letter, a note.
Structured | Blocks organised in sections of different levels.  The first
 | block of each section is its headline.  A report, a proposal, a book.
.end

An unstructured document has no commands for sections.  Append offers
Column, Block and Global-block; Split offers Column and Block; Join offers
Block; Copy, Cut, Move and Delete ask for Text, Column or Block.  The keys
that move through the outline ({k:Shift-Up}, {k:Shift-Down}, {k:Shift-PgUp},
{k:Shift-PgDn} and the outline control keys) belong to a structured document;
in an unstructured one the outline control keys show the message
"Collapse/expand works in structured documents".  The Cross-reference marker
appears in parentheses on the Marker Commands menu and cannot be chosen.

A structured document organizes blocks in sections.  The outline shows its
shape and the full text shows its content; a cut section can be pasted
elsewhere, and the numbers of all the sections change by themselves.
Cross-references, numbered headlines and tables of contents need a structured
document.

.h3 Making a document structured or unstructured [view-make-kind]
.ix New document, structured
.ix New document, unstructured
.ix Edit, Structured
.ix Edit, Unstructured
A prompt of the Document Manager offers Structured or Unstructured when a
document that does not exist yet is edited.  The command File, New takes the
kind from
the template named in its Template field (the template DEFAULT.SET unless
another is named).  A document that exists is structured or unstructured as
it was saved.

The Edit menu changes the kind of an open document.
{b:Edit, Structured} (offered in an unstructured document) turns the whole
text into one section, whose first block becomes the headline, and the status
line then shows {c:STRUCTURED EDIT}.  {b:Edit, Unstructured} (offered
in a structured document that is a single section with nothing below it)
turns it back.  A document with several sections cannot be made
unstructured: the entry is not on the menu.  A change of the kind counts as
a change of the document: Quit puts the question whether to save it.

.h3 Editing one section [view-section-mode]
.ix Edit, Section
.ix Edit, Document
.ix Ctrl-^
.ix Section edit
.ix SECTION EDIT
In a structured document the command {b:Edit, Section} (or {k:Ctrl-^}) makes
the Editor show only the section that holds the cursor, with its
subsections, as if it were the whole document.  The rest of the text is out
of sight and out of reach: a search, a Go To or a count deals with the
section only, and the status line shows {c:SECTION EDIT}.  The entry of the
menu then reads {b:Edit, Document}; it brings the whole text back, as does
{k:Ctrl-^} again.  {k:Esc} does not leave the mode.  The mode is not part of
the file.

.pic pictures/view-section-edit.png 5.4 Edit, Section: one section shown alone.

.h2 Guidelines for large documents [view-guidelines]
.ix Large documents, guidelines
.ix Guidelines
.ix Document, large
.ix Master document
.ix Include marker, several authors
A document of a hundred pages is easier to write, to revise and to print
when it follows these guidelines.  The table gives the reason for each.

.table 20 40
Guideline | Why
Make the document structured. | You can see its outline, rearrange it by
 | moving sections, and number it automatically.
Keep blocks short, about a | A short block is quick to move and to
 page at most. | format.
Give a block one paragraph. | Cut, copy and move then take a paragraph
 | as a unit, and a format applies to it alone.
Set the format globally first. | A setting made once in the Global menu
 | holds for the whole document.  Use local
 | settings only for exceptions.
Use named formats. | A block format that you use again and again
 | can be given a name (Global, Named) and changed
 | in one place.
Work in one section at a time. | Edit, Section ({k:Ctrl-^}) hides the rest, so
 | that searches and counts stay within it.
Keep the headlines meaningful. | The outline ({k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9}) shows only
 | headlines; a good headline is a good table of
 | contents.
Share the work in parts. | Only one person can edit a document at a time;
 | with the Include marker each author has a
 | document of their own for a part.
Save often. | {k:Ctrl-S} saves at once.  Autosave and a backup
 | file are there, but they do not replace it.
.end

.caution A structured document that is edited as one large unbroken
  block is slow to lay out and awkward to move around in.  Divide long
  stretches of text into blocks.

.h3 A work with several authors [view-master]
.ix Master document
.ix Several authors
.ix Include marker
Strata edits, searches and prints a document of thousands of pages as one
document.  A document cannot be edited by two people at the same time.  For a
work with several authors, give each author a document for a part and make a
master document that holds an Include marker for each part (Marker, Include;
see {n:marker-include}).  Printing the master prints the parts together, with
continuous numbering.  {x:authors} describes the setup.

.h3 Saving, backup and autosave [view-save-habits]
.ix Autosave
.ix Backup file
.ix Save, habits
.ix Backups Kept
Three mechanisms protect the work.  Save ({k:Ctrl-S}) writes the document
under its name at once.  The setting Backups Kept (Setup, or Global, Options)
keeps the previous versions of the file under numbered names such as
{c:.backup/report.bak01}, in a directory next to the file; Restore Backup of
the Document Manager brings one back.  Autosave saves a document that has
unsaved changes when the interval has passed since the first change (120
seconds by default); the interval and the on-off setting are in the Setup
panel of the Document Manager.  Autosave saves only a document that already
has a name.  See {x:file} for the details.

.h2 Global and local formats [view-formatting]
.ix Format, global
.ix Format, local
.ix Global format
.ix Local format
.ix Override, hierarchy of formats
.ix Named format
.ix Format settings
Strata separates the typing of text from its formatting.  The screen is an
approximation; the Printer does the real formatting, with fonts and pages,
when the document is printed or previewed.  The formats are the settings that
control how the text looks: justification, indents, tabs, attribute, fonts
and page layout.  They are global or local.

.h3 Global format settings [view-global-formats]
.ix Global menu
.ix Global, Format
.ix Global, Page
.ix Global, Typeface
.ix Global, Marker
.ix Global, Options
.ix Global, Named
A global setting holds for the whole document: for all the headline blocks
and all the text blocks of a structured document, or all the blocks of an
unstructured one.  The Global menu of the {k:F12} bar makes them; its entries
are Format, Page, Typeface, Marker, Options and Named.  The global settings
are the attributes, the justification and the indents of the blocks
(separately for headlines and for text, and, for the headlines, separately for
each level), the page layout, the typefaces, the way the markers are shown
and the options of the Editor.  A global setting stays in force until a local
setting overrides it.  {X:format} describes every panel and every field of the
Global menu.

.h3 Local format settings [view-local-formats]
.ix Local format, text
.ix Local format, column
.ix Local format, block
.ix Block Format Editor
.ix F6
.ix Ctrl-E
.ix Hanging indent
.ix Ctrl-H
A local setting affects one element of the document: some text, a column or
a block.  It takes precedence over the global settings.  For example, a
block that holds a quotation can be set to print in italics; the setting
overrides the global attribute for that block alone.

The settings form a hierarchy.  The most local is the attribute of the text
itself; no other setting overrides it.  The order of precedence is: the text,
the column, the block, the named format that the block uses, the global
setting for the level (for a headline) and the global setting of the document.
The Editor takes the first setting of that order that exists.

.table 6 15 37
Order | Setting | How it is made
1 | Text | An attribute: {k:Ctrl-B}, {k:Alt-I}, {k:Ctrl-U}, {k:Ctrl-N},
 | | {k:Alt-F6}.
2 | Column | The Block Format Editor, {k:F6} or {k:Ctrl-E}, in a block
 | | that has several columns.
3 | Block | The same, in a one column block; {k:Ctrl-H} for a
 | | hanging indent.
4 | Named format | Global, Named, and the name given to a block.
5 | Level | Global, Format, for the headlines of a level.
6 | Global | Global, Format, Page, Typeface, Marker, Options.
.end

{k:F6} (also {k:Ctrl-E}) opens the Block Format Editor, a short series of
prompts about the block that holds the cursor: the left block indent, the
justification (Left, Right, Center or Even), the indent of the lines
(None, First, All but First, All), the attribute (Normal, Bold, Italics,
Bold Italics or Underscore Words) and the tabs, typed as a list such as
{c:5,20R,40D}, where a letter after the position gives the type of the
tab (L left, R right, C center, D decimal, a comma for comma, N for number)
and {c:0} means none.  {X:format} describes the Block Format Editor and the
settings.  After a local setting is made the block shows it at once on the
screen: the justification and the tabs are laid out, and the ruler shows the
tabs of the block that holds the cursor.

.h3 The expanded status line and the ruler show the format [view-format-view]
The format that applies to the cursor is visible all the time.  The ruler
under the status line shows the left indent and the tabs of the block that
holds the cursor, and the expanded status line (Global, Options, Expanded
Status Line?  Yes) shows the justification, the indent, the left block indent
and whether the block is a headline.  {x:view-screen} describes both.
